WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.090
JOHN MURRAY: Welcome,
everybody, to Take

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.060
Control of your Multi-cluster,
Multi-Tenant Kubernetes

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.450
Workloads.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.870
This is a talk we're
going to give today

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:21.850
about dealing with Kubernetes
in Enterprise deployments

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.020
where you've got lots of
clusters, lots of tenants.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:31.690
And in particular, we're going
to talk about the GKE Policy

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:35.190
Management alpha that
we announced here today.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.720
And then we'll be
demoing that in the end.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.310
My name is John Murray.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.890
I'm a product manager
with Google Cloud.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.960
With me is going
to be Ray Colline,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.820
who's one of the lead
engineers on the project.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.624
So upfront, we're going to go
through some of the principles

00:00:52.624 --> 00:00:54.040
that we were looking
at when we're

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.290
trying to deal with
some of the problems

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.680
around managing multiple
Kubernetes clusters

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.350
and dealing with the
multi-tenancy issues that arise

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.620
from that when you're thinking
about policies, access

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.320
control, quota limitations,
and those sorts of things.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.970
So we'll walk through that.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.040
Ray will spend some time
walking through the architecture

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.900
and some of the
features that we have

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.060
that we're debuting
here in the alpha.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.828
And then we'll do a
demo for you at the end.

00:01:22.828 --> 00:01:27.320
So to start it off, I want to
talk about, just generally,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.890
the problems that we see
when we talk to customers

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:35.120
who are really looking to scale
up their Kubernetes deployment.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.190
And these are
administrative problems.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.900
They're the problems
of operators

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.410
in dealing with, how do you get
a bunch of development teams

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.420
onto Kubernetes infrastructure?

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.760
How do you make them
productive while ensuring

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.480
that things are secure and that
you're keeping an eye on all

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.620
of the administrative things?

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.420
So when people start
out with Kubernetes,

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.730
this is usually what we see.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.510
These sort of cluster per
tenant scenarios, where

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.840
you have one tenant,
in this case, a dev

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.759
team, the blue
team, and they want

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.259
to play around with
Kubernetes, they

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:11.210
want to experiment with it, so
they go and spin-up a cluster.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:14.410
And that's very easy on
GKE, but it's also very easy

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.880
to spin it up using some tooling
or to work with any other cloud

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.020
provider.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.290
And once you have multiple
people doing this,

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.530
now you have a number of these
cluster per tenant scenarios

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.780
within your company.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.580
So you have one tenant,
they have their own cluster,

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.890
they start running their pods
in the default namespace,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.650
and that's fine.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.100
They have isolation,
because that cluster

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.590
is the boundary for them,
and it's not necessarily

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.220
an administrative hassle.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.770
But if this scales,
and you've got

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.780
100 of these clusters running
with 100 different tenants,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.820
all running their
clusters different ways,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.490
then it's hard to think about
normalizing things like access

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.000
control across them.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.934
It's hard to deal with upgrades.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:55.350
It's hard to deal
with versioning.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.230
It's hard to deal with all the
sorts of administrative tasks

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.800
that you have to manage.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.010
So the next phase
of the evolution

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.130
that we see people go through
is that they take that tenant

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.990
and they provision
them a namespace

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.120
across multiple clusters.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.670
And so now you've got, say,
three clusters running.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.240
Maybe you're doing that
because you need clusters

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.020
in each region close to where
your users or your workloads

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:21.560
are.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.390
Maybe you're doing
it for redundancy.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.130
There's a number
of reasons why you

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.501
might want multiple clusters.

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:29.125
If we give a namespace
to the blue team

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:30.583
in each one of
those clusters, then

00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:32.410
they can run their pods there.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:37.510
And we can attach policies, and
access control, quota control,

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.135
network policies, et
cetera, at the namespace

00:03:40.135 --> 00:03:43.700
or at the cluster levels
to kind of normalize that.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:48.800
Where it really gets tricky is
when you have multiple tenants

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.880
across multiple clusters.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.970
So in this case, I've got
two tenants, a blue team

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.870
and a green team, each
with their own namespace

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.610
in all my clusters.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.260
But in a real life
scenario, you may

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.570
have hundreds of
these tenant teams,

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.370
each with their own
namespace, and you may

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.320
have dozens of these clusters.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.250
And so the scalability
problem becomes, then,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.520
how do you manage the
policies across all of these?

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.850
Because each of these
Kubernetes clusters

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.410
is somewhat of an
island when you're

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.240
talking about
policy management--

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.550
setting up the
namespace definitions,

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.890
setting up role-based
access controls for each

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.930
of these namespaces,
and ensuring

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.390
that each of the namespaces
looks the same across all

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.760
the clusters so that your
development teams don't

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.490
have to deal with variance
across that workspace.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.870
And today, people are managing
that in a number of ways,

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:40.430
but it's a lot.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.030
And so we see it over and
over again, as people scale up

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.950
from experimenting
through Kubernetes

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.980
to dealing with it at
scale, this is a point

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.440
that they end up with.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.540
And maybe they try to
manage it with scripts,

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:55.466
maybe they build
their own tooling,

00:04:55.466 --> 00:04:57.590
but it's something that we
wanted to take a look at

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.170
and say, can we,
as Google, come up

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.270
with something for the
Kubernetes community

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.017
that helps deal with
this particular problem?

00:05:05.017 --> 00:05:07.600
So that's where we're going to
talk about with you guys today.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.620
Before we get into the
nitty gritty of the details

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.380
about what we've built and
what we're doing in the alpha,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.680
I just wanted to talk about it
a couple of design principles

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.870
that we used when we
were looking through how

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.280
to deal with this problem.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.470
So the first one is that we
didn't want Kubernetes clusters

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.970
to be islands.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.360
Right now there's very
little federation support

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.680
in Kubernetes.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.890
And so if you're running
dozens of clusters,

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.470
you're often interacting
with them one by one,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.050
loading them all up into
the context of your kubectl,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:44.250
and switching around,
scripting against that.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.220
But automation here
is really the only way

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.830
to manage this in a sane way
across a lot of clusters.

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.680
So we wanted to
build tooling that

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.960
works in this
cross-cluster environment

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.850
and provides some
centralization so

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.190
that you don't repeat
yourself over and over again

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.762
when applying policies.

00:06:01.762 --> 00:06:03.220
The other thing we
wanted to do was

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.460
create a clear separation
between infrastructure

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.850
and tenancy.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.500
So the analogy here is
like an apartment building.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.250
You start with an empty
apartment building

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.770
like an empty cluster,
and you need to set it up

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.300
that you can bring
people in and have

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.550
them move into the apartments.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.594
And that's things
like setting up

00:06:20.594 --> 00:06:22.010
the infrastructure--
the plumbing,

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.460
the heating, the electricity.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.680
But it's also setting
up a lease, right?

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.480
What are the terms
of your tenancy?

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.190
What are you allowed to do?

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.950
You're not allowed to play death
metal at 2:00 in the morning.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:37.300
You're not allowed to hang
out naked in the hallways.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.530
You need to be a good tenant.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:42.336
But within the constraints
of your apartment,

00:06:42.336 --> 00:06:43.960
you're allowed to do
whatever you want.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.860
You can hang up whatever
posters you want.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.790
We're not going to tell you
what couch color you need to do.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.320
We create a safe
environment for you

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.020
where things are
clearly delineated.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.540
The administrative things
are for administrators,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.940
and dev teams are freed
up to do what they

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.680
need to do in an agile way.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.220
And the last principle
is policy workflow.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.960
So we can automate
policies, we can centralize

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.380
policy management,
but we want to make

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.430
the actual application of
those policies easy to manage.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:18.070
And oftentimes, when you're
talking about scaled up

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.044
infrastructure,
you've got a team

00:07:20.044 --> 00:07:22.210
of people who are doing
administrative stuff setting

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.320
policies.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:24.940
And so we wanted
to build workflow

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.090
around the changes that
happen around the definition

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.810
of those policies, so that
administrators could cooperate

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:35.970
with one another,
review policy changes,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.720
think about policy
changes in the same way

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.070
that you think about code.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.990
You can roll them back.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.790
You can do reviews.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:46.540
You can edit them, and have
auditability around changes

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.560
that are made there--

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.060
a lot of the things
that we take for

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.160
granted when we're talking
about source control.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.419
We wanted to bring some of that
goodness to policy management.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:55.960
So those things we
are thinking about

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.180
as we were looking at
building something.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.410
We spent the last couple of
months here getting it ready

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.630
and bringing it out to alpha.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:08.112
So please welcome to the stage
Ray Colline, my colleague.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:09.820
He's an engineer on
the project, and he's

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:15.574
going to walk you through
Kubernetes Policy Management.

00:08:15.574 --> 00:08:17.440
RAY COLLINE: Thanks.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.150
Thank you, John.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.100
And again, I'm Ray
Colline, and I'm

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.040
a software engineer who
thinks a lot about policy

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.669
management on Kubernetes.

00:08:26.669 --> 00:08:29.210
So today we're going to actually
be getting in to talk about,

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.610
what did we do for this alpha?

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.650
And what are we going to be
doing going forward with this?

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:33.940
And where do we see it going?

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:36.591
And how, hopefully,
it can help you.

00:08:36.591 --> 00:08:38.049
So the first thing
I want to say is

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:40.780
that this is just one part
of a much bigger story

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.690
that we've presented
here at Next.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:46.630
And importantly, with
the hybrid story,

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.970
you'll see here that policy
management, which is down

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.820
at the bottom, fits into
the overall on-prem, GKE,

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:55.690
managed Istio.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.940
We want to have a way of
dealing with all these things

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.760
from a policy perspective,
or as the analogy

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.467
that John used, thinking
about the building owner

00:09:03.467 --> 00:09:05.050
in this case who's
trying to lease out

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:10.610
these apartments which have all
of these particular features.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:14.240
So when we think about policy
management specifically,

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.630
we're thinking about
centrally defined policies

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:18.860
in a source of truth.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.470
So you want one place
to go look at that you

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.500
can say, here is all of the
tenants in my environment

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.440
across all of my
clusters, and know that

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.010
when you submit and put
changes in that source of truth

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:34.550
that it applies across
all of your clusters.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:36.200
And so we're really
doubling down

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:40.130
as namespaces in Kubernetes
as the minimum level

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:42.152
of tenancy for defining this.

00:09:42.152 --> 00:09:43.610
And you can think
about it in terms

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.190
of the analogy is that
namespaces are really

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.750
those apartments that
you're leasing out.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.560
And so with this
central policy store,

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.210
you can manage things like RBAC,
and quota policy, and more.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:10:00.350
And that more is coming.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.480
So I mentioned the idea
of sources of truth.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.540
What I'm going to
try to do now is

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:10.760
detail how the components of
GKE Policy Management work,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.580
and the concepts that
we've introduced.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.500
So it starts with
the idea of a source

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:17.120
of truth, which is
that place where

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.981
your policies are defined.

00:10:18.981 --> 00:10:20.480
Now, in the alpha,
I'm going to talk

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.480
about two particular sources
of truth that we support today,

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.730
but the intention is
to be able to build

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:30.044
arbitrary sources of truth to
meet customers where they are.

00:10:30.044 --> 00:10:31.460
And we know a lot
of our customers

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.289
end up creating their
own policy stores,

00:10:33.289 --> 00:10:35.330
and they want to figure
out how to make that work

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:36.894
with their hybrid environments.

00:10:36.894 --> 00:10:38.810
And so, we've designed
that with this in mind,

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.990
where one's going to be able
to write a component to read

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.000
arbitrary sources of truth.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.400
And so what we've enabled
in the source of truth

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.300
is the concept of hierarchy.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.950
So now, today in Kubernetes,
namespaces are flat

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.790
and then there's a
cluster on top of it.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.820
And you can basically
set a cluster-wide policy

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.950
or you can set a per
namespace policy.

00:10:57.950 --> 00:10:59.780
But in many
organizations, they want

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:02.360
to actually group namespaces
and then apply policies

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.500
to a subset of namespaces.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.310
So in GKE Policy Management
we support the concept

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:10.322
of a group of namespaces that
have common RBAC policies

00:11:10.322 --> 00:11:11.405
and common quota policies.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.120
So we have that as
the source of truth.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.810
And then within each cluster--

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.370
so every cluster that's
being managed by GKE policy

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:25.100
management, there's a set of
components and controllers

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:30.480
that we install as part of
GKE Policy Management install.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:34.374
So I'm going to first talk about
the idea of a policy importer.

00:11:34.374 --> 00:11:36.290
And so what the policy
importer is responsible

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.630
for is reaching out to
that source of truth,

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.250
whether it be Git,
whether it be GCP,

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:44.240
whether it be a MySQL database,
and reading the policy data

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.500
and putting it into the
cluster as a set of CRDs,

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.750
or Kubernetes,
basically, objects

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.220
that represent the data.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.440
And then there's the
syncer component,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.280
which reads those and is able
to talk to the Kubernetes API

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.240
server to create those objects.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.320
So we would manage things
like namespaces, RBAC policy,

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:06.530
resource quota objects,
possibly limit ranges--

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:08.060
there's a bunch of objects.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.660
And the idea of that separation
is that the policy importer

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:12.660
knows how to speak to
different sources of truth

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.010
and normalizes it to a
format we understand,

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:17.720
and then the syncer
reads that format

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:20.300
and realizes the
changes on the cluster.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.210
And then I mentioned that we
support hierarchical quota.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.150
Well, again, inside
of Kubernetes

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:26.630
today there is no hierarchy.

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:28.220
It's just flat namespaces.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.350
So we wrote our own
admission controller

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.950
which understands hierarchy and
can support oversubscription.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.980
And so now when you
create pods and whatnot,

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.830
you actually can control
the number of CPUs

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.560
at a group of namespaces level.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.310
So now I'm going to
talk a little bit more

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.220
about our sources of truth
that we shipped in the alpha.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.680
So in the alpha,
we have Git and we

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.260
have GCP as a source of truth.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.140
And so I'm going to
start talking about Git.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.490
And the reason we chose Git,
is because GitOps turns out

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.114
to be a way that a
lot of organizations

00:12:58.114 --> 00:13:00.522
think about managing policy.

00:13:00.522 --> 00:13:01.980
And the advantages
here get to some

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:05.130
of the idea of the workflow
that John spoke about.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:08.160
Code tooling, like Git, supports
the idea of pre-commits.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.260
You can do continuous
integration.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.210
You can do code reviews.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.880
And so now you can think
of creating an RBAC

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.390
policy as a proposal
or a merge request,

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.700
and then someone
then can approve it,

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.030
and at once it gets integrated.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.404
And then once it
gets integrated,

00:13:23.404 --> 00:13:26.400
we automatically sync it down
to the cluster and apply it.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.380
So this lets you use a
workflow and introduce

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.380
arbitrary levels of
policy and metapolicy

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.800
into your management.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.940
So specifically with
the Git source of truth,

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.120
we've created our
own directory format

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.070
which represents the hierarchy.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.120
And effectively, we
use the file system,

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:47.982
which is a very good, natural
way to represent hierarchy.

00:13:47.982 --> 00:13:49.440
And you'll see here
in this example

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.780
on the right, where you
have Foo Corp, which is

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.520
the root of the organization.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.440
And then underneath
it you have online,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.090
and online's a department.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:00.510
And within online, you can
have a series of namespaces,

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.560
and you also can have
an RBAC binding YAML.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.950
And so, what we do is we use the
same YAML you use in kubectl.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:09.450
So it's the same
exact type of thing

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.970
you'd write if you
were writing kubectl,

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:14.010
except you're checking
it into a repo.

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.010
And because it
has the hierarchy,

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:18.090
we'll actually take
those RBAC policies,

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:20.920
and we flatten them to create
the bindings in each namespace,

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:22.530
giving the online
admin the ability

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:26.910
to see shipping app backend,
and shipping prod, et cetera.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:32.510
Now, what we also have is
the concept of a vetting tool

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.910
that makes sure that the
integrity of this file system

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:35.450
stays right.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.170
So if you check in random
stuff-- for example,

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:39.200
if you create a directory
under a namespace--

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:40.640
that's not allowed.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.160
So for us, what's really
important is the question of,

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:45.800
will it blend, or will it apply?

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.199
The answer should always be yes.

00:14:47.199 --> 00:14:49.490
And every time that question
is asked, it's always yes.

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:51.627
So this is a really big
deal in policy as code,

00:14:51.627 --> 00:14:53.210
because you don't
want to check things

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.765
in that get to the end
of the pipeline and fail.

00:14:55.765 --> 00:14:57.140
Because when that
happens, you're

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:59.514
now stuck with a situation
where your Git source of truth

00:14:59.514 --> 00:15:01.634
no longer represents the
reality on the clusters.

00:15:01.634 --> 00:15:03.050
And then the
operator right there,

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:05.120
if they don't do anything,
that stays persistent.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:07.580
And then you don't have a
good idea of what's what,

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:10.080
or you have to revert to change
and figure out what happens.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.460
So we spent a bunch of time
creating a vetting tool that

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:15.740
makes sure that the integrity
of that tree is done,

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:17.990
and that's part of the alpha.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.790
So anyways, once you commit,
this stuff is connected to

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:25.420
and grabbed each cluster,
and the policies are applied.

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.706
OK.

00:15:27.706 --> 00:15:29.830
Now I want to talk about
our other source of truth,

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.640
which is GCP.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:33.680
So within GCP there
is a hierarchy,

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.255
and it's defined through
the Cloud Resource Manager.

00:15:36.255 --> 00:15:37.630
So the Cloud
Resource Manager has

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.940
the concept of an organization,
which is the root.

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:43.720
It has GCP folders, which are
basically sub organizations,

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:45.060
and projects.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:48.520
Now, projects in GCP are the
minimum level of tenancy.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:51.567
And namespaces in Kubernetes are
the minimum level of tenancy.

00:15:51.567 --> 00:15:53.650
So they have a really nice
relationship one-to-one

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:54.980
with each other.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:57.850
And so what we've done
here is we've said, OK,

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:01.030
with an API that
we're also putting out

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:04.720
with the alpha launch, you
can associate a namespace

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:05.970
with a project.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:09.160
So now what happens is, is
that if you IM policies,

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.370
and you use Cloud for IM,
and you set it on a project,

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.020
and you associate that
project with a namespace,

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:18.940
we will synchronize the IM
policy to an RBAC policy

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.300
on the cluster.

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:23.030
So you can manage all of
your access control policies,

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:24.700
whether it be for
using Cloud SQL,

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:28.660
or whether it's using
Kubernetes, in the same place.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:30.430
And this even works
whether you're

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:33.070
using GKE or using
Kubernetes on GCE,

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.590
or even using Amazon, or
another on-prem cloud provider.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:40.540
You can use the GCP
hierarchy with IM

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:43.110
to manage those workloads.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.610
And so what this
thing does is, one,

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.720
it converts IM policies to RBAC.

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.460
But also, it does
the compilation

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:50.440
using the GCP Cloud
Resource Manager

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:53.500
hierarchy to provide the
ability to do the subdivision

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.700
of namespace management.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.330
So with that, I'm
going to take you

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:02.450
back to John who will do a
demonstration of what I just

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:03.032
talked about.

00:17:03.032 --> 00:17:03.698
JOHN MURRAY: OK.

00:17:03.698 --> 00:17:04.940
RAY COLLINE: Thanks, John.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.170
JOHN MURRAY: Before
I get into the demo,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.810
I just want to set the
stage a little bit.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.859
So Ray showed you a Git
example a little bit earlier.

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:15.650
That's what I'm going
to walk through here,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:19.160
and just kind of show you how
I define some namespaces, how

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.890
I set up tenants so
that they can deploy

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:23.880
their pods on the clusters.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:27.230
We'll talk a little bit
about controlling quota,

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:31.310
particularly controlling
quota across namespaces.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:34.700
And then we'll set up a new
namespace for a new tenant just

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:36.230
to show you what
that looks like.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:39.077
We'll do it across a
couple of clusters.

00:17:39.077 --> 00:17:40.910
The example we're going
to walk through here

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:44.600
is Foo Corp. Foo Corp
has, today, right now,

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.030
an online division,
and the online division

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:48.770
has dev, staging,
and prod set up

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.690
as namespaces on
all the clusters.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.800
That's the policy file that
we'll be applying here.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:58.020
And then we've got policies
attached at different levels

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:00.270
of the hierarchy here.

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:03.380
Up at the top we've
got admin policies

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:06.620
so that we can view the
pods, set pod security policy

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.440
across the entire organization.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.440
And then down at the level
of the namespace tenants,

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:16.610
we'll define in name spaces
themselves, set up some shared

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:20.660
resource quota, as well
as namespace level quota,

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:22.160
and we'll set up
a pod creator role

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:25.500
so our dev team can show up
and do what they do best,

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.740
which is deploy applications.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:32.130
Can we cut over to the demo?

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:33.540
OK, great.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.750
So what you're seeing
here is a tmux session.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.190
I've got a watch
on the right side.

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.670
That's a local repository,
or local version

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:42.810
of the repository.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:46.380
It's actually hosted
on GitHub, and it

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:49.710
shows the basic
high level policies

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:53.620
that I've got applied
across my company right now.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:55.800
So none of the namespace
structure is in there.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:59.730
We don't need any namespaces
specific to our development.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.700
I'm watching two clusters
here at the bottom,

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.920
just listing out the namespaces.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.660
And that's just the basic stuff
that comes with Kubernetes,

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.590
as well as our agents
that are installed locally

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:12.060
on the clusters.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.275
So let's get started.

00:19:17.110 --> 00:19:19.600
I'm going to look
at this cluster

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:23.065
here on the left with kubectl.

00:19:23.065 --> 00:19:25.620
And now I'm going to bring
in a bunch of policies.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:28.280
So I'm just copying
these locally from one

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:30.544
directory to another
on my machine.

00:19:30.544 --> 00:19:32.210
And you see from the
tree that now we're

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:36.470
going to add this online
division to Foo Corp.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:39.200
Underneath online
there, you've got

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:43.370
shipping app backend,
which is our service, which

00:19:43.370 --> 00:19:48.800
has three namespaces--
dev, prod, and staging.

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:50.810
All those have
namespace definitions,

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:55.130
so they're just standard YAML,
as well as some role binding.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:57.470
So we've got the pod
creator role binding here.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:00.410
And you notice that's at a
level above the namespaces.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:03.530
So that pod creator role
binding will apply to all three

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:06.020
of those namespaces.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:07.960
And the same is true
of the quota there.

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:11.240
So we'll be enforcing that quota
as a shared pool of resources

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:13.527
across the three namespaces.

00:20:13.527 --> 00:20:15.860
And then we've got kind of
namespace level role bindings

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:18.890
here for job creator, and
a namespace level rule

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:21.050
defined there.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:22.800
So that's the
initial policy state

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.770
that I want on my clusters.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:29.150
I'm going to go ahead and make
a commit here up to GitHub

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:36.120
to initialize some of those
policies, and push it out.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:39.570
OK, so that's going up to
the cloud, being applied.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:43.020
And you can see there, locally,
cluster 1 picked it up,

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:45.510
and cluster 2 is a
couple of seconds behind,

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:46.860
but it also picked it up.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:51.300
So what happened there is
the pods that we've installed

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.640
with our system on these
individual clusters

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:56.490
are constantly pulling
that repository,

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:59.950
pulling down the whole thing,
interpreting the hierarchy

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:03.000
there, flattening it out
into Kubernetes namespaces,

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.280
and making all the
policy applications.

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:09.880
So now I've got all of
my namespaces set up,

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:12.700
let's go take a look at it.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:15.190
So if I look at one of
these role bindings--

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:18.460
and this is the pod
creator role binding here

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:20.920
that I've got set up at
an intermediate level

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:23.010
of the shipping app backend.

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:27.210
If I look at that definition,
it's pretty simple.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:28.860
It's giving me a
pod creator a role,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:32.940
which allows me to deploy
pods in the cluster.

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:34.740
And we're binding
that to our developer

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.140
here, Alice, at
Foo Corp. So that's

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.540
going to give Alice the ability
to do whatever she needs

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:45.270
to do to manage her application
across those three namespaces.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:49.170
If you look at the actual
role bindings on the cluster,

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:56.110
you can see in shipping dev that
it's got three role bindings.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:59.400
So this is the flattened
out version of the hierarchy

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.530
that we have in our repository.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:03.750
It's got the Foo
Corp viewers, which

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:06.450
is up at the top, defined.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.310
It's got a pod
creator role binding

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:10.100
that we were just talking about.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:13.710
And it's got a
namespace-specific role binding

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.000
in job creators.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:17.580
So that's what our system is
doing is flattening that out

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:21.360
and applying it to all the name
spaces across all the clusters.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:24.660
And if we want to test that
out, we can pretend we're Alice

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:28.781
and try to get some
secrets out of Kubernetes.

00:22:28.781 --> 00:22:30.780
Pod creator role binding
does not give me access

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:33.420
to those secrets,
and so that fails.

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:37.400
But if I try to get the
pods, that succeeds.

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:39.750
And now I don't have
any pods, but it

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:43.560
didn't fail the off-check.

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.980
So let's look at the quota.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:50.030
This is the quota file that's
in shipping app backend.

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:52.400
So it's up there at
the intermediate level

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:53.900
of the hierarchy.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:55.790
And this is a quota
definition that

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:58.280
is shared by these
three namespaces.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.800
It's a pretty
simple quota YAML--

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:10.540
three pods, one core,
and one gig of memory.

00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:11.430
So let's test that.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.000
I'm going to create a pod
here with my exciting new

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:19.890
application BusyBox Sleep, and
I'm going to ask for about 80%

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:23.260
of a processor.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:24.930
So that goes
through, no problem.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.000
It's less than the shared quota.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:29.850
And now I'm eating
up 80% of that shared

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:35.980
quota with a deployment in
the namespace shipping prod.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:39.450
So what happens if I want to
deploy in one of the sister

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:43.350
namespaces here, shipping dev?

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:46.690
Same image, but now I'm
going to ask for 30%.

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:50.760
So I asked for 80% before,
I'm asking for 30% now.

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:53.160
For those of you who haven't
been able to do the math,

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:58.450
that's more than one CPU,
and so this should fail.

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:00.030
And it does.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:01.860
And what's happening
here is we're

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.410
evaluating the quota
at the shipping dev

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:07.050
level, which passes.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.360
And then we're going up
a level in the hierarchy

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.250
and serially evaluating
the quota that's

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.650
shared across all three
of these namespaces,

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:15.300
and that's where this fails.

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:18.750
And so, you can set quota in
the hierarchy at an arbitrary

00:24:18.750 --> 00:24:22.740
number of levels here, and
we'll enforce it up the chain.

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:25.590
And we think this is a pretty
cool way to deal with the fact

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.860
that Kubernetes only
natively manages resource

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:31.320
quota at the namespace level.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.870
If you have sub t
namespace, but you

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:36.490
don't want to deal with
portioning out pools of quota

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:41.770
across them, this is one
way you can accomplish that.

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.710
And just to show you that
it does actually work,

00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:46.600
we'll try the same
application here,

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:49.404
but we'll ask for only 10%.

00:24:49.404 --> 00:24:50.320
And that goes through.

00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:54.000
So that's quota.

00:24:56.910 --> 00:24:58.382
Let's set up a new namespace.

00:24:58.382 --> 00:25:00.090
So let's say we've
got a new dev team who

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:03.030
wants to come on to the
Kubernetes deployments

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:08.410
that we've got, and they want
a home on all of our clusters.

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:09.580
That's fairly simple here.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:11.288
All I have to do is
make them a directory

00:25:11.288 --> 00:25:12.520
and define them a namespace.

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.370
So I'm going to make
a directory locally,

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:17.844
which you should see
pop into my tree here.

00:25:17.844 --> 00:25:19.260
So this is shipping
app front-end,

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.715
and we'll give the
front-end team a home.

00:25:21.715 --> 00:25:23.340
Now, that's an empty
directory, so I've

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:26.180
got to give them a
namespace definition.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:30.180
And we'll just set up a simple
namespace for them in YAML.

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:32.640
So I've got my directory.

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:33.930
I've got my YAML.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:36.210
And I'm going to commit that
and push it up the cloud.

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:49.300
And if you're looking at the
bottom there at my clusters,

00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:51.530
they should pick up that change.

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:55.390
And you'll see the shipping
app front-end namespace there.

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:57.040
Six seconds, five seconds--

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:58.870
just showing up on the clusters.

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:01.330
The great thing about this is
I'm only running two clusters

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:05.710
here, but because all the
clusters are pulling down this,

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:09.900
you can run this system
with one central repository.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:12.910
And as long as that
repository can handle the QPS,

00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:17.270
you can scale this out to as
many clusters as you want.

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:22.100
So let's take a look at
some of these role bindings

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:23.767
for shipping app front-end.

00:26:23.767 --> 00:26:25.600
So you can see it's got
a flattened out role

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:27.657
binding from Foo Corp viewers.

00:26:27.657 --> 00:26:29.240
That's fine, but
it's not going to let

00:26:29.240 --> 00:26:31.850
anybody on the development
team do anything.

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:34.844
So we need to set up a
role binding for Alice,

00:26:34.844 --> 00:26:36.260
our developer, so
that she can get

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:38.750
into the front-end namespace
and start deploying some

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.790
of our front-end applications.

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.480
So the way I'm going to do
that is not to go and define

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:46.580
a separate role
binding for Alice,

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:49.250
because she's already got a
role binding for pod creation.

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:51.680
What I'm going to do is take
the existing role binding--

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:54.080
so that's the one that's
shipping app backend here,

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:56.050
this pod creator role binding--

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:58.620
and I'm just going to move that
up a level in the hierarchy.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:00.578
So I'm going to take that
binding and just say,

00:27:00.578 --> 00:27:03.170
OK, actually, Alice should have
pod creation across everything

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:06.426
below online in my tree.

00:27:06.426 --> 00:27:08.810
And so if I just move
that up one directory

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:11.300
by moving the file
around, that should

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:17.460
be sufficient to give Alice
the privileges that she needs.

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:18.620
So I'll push that change.

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:26.100
And then if I go look
at the role bindings

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:29.770
on shipping app front-end,
I should see, hopefully,

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:33.030
Alice's new permission pop in.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:36.154
And there it is-- four seconds
ago, online pod creators.

00:27:36.154 --> 00:27:38.070
And so just by moving
that role abiding around

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:41.460
in my repository, now it
applies to a whole different set

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:44.120
of namespaces and my
developers are good to go.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:47.220
Alice can go and
do what she needs

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:49.090
to do in terms of deployment.

00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:56.810
So that's the basics of this.

00:27:56.810 --> 00:28:00.090
You see the quota can be managed
across different hierarchies.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:05.150
You can see a sane way to
define things once and do it

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:10.030
in a way that fits the
hierarchy of larger teams.

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:13.130
And doing things
in the Git way here

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:15.170
really allows you to do
the GitOps experience

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:20.090
of making these commits,
validating them, potentially

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:23.660
doing a pull request and a
review, and kind of sanely

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:25.850
checking that
across team members

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:27.830
to make sure that
everybody's happy with them

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:30.500
before they're applied and
pulled down to the clusters.

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:32.000
And then you're
only doing it once.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:35.610
And because the automation
that's available here,

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:37.910
as well as some of the
monitoring and logging that we

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:41.300
have that lets you know that
changes actually get propagate,

00:28:41.300 --> 00:28:42.770
you only have to do it once.

00:28:42.770 --> 00:28:46.370
And if you have made
a mistake, if you

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:50.280
did apply some policies that
are problematic for you,

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.680
all you have to do is roll back.

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:53.554
We've got the full
history here, because it's

00:28:53.554 --> 00:28:56.180
in a code repository, so I
can revert all those changes

00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:00.530
that I made, roll them back,
and push that up to the cloud.

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:03.822
And I'm back where I started.

00:29:03.822 --> 00:29:05.780
It'll kill all of my
namespaces because they're

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:08.780
managed by [? Nomos, ?] get
rid of all those deployments,

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:13.820
and put me back into a state
that allows me to roll back

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:20.080
to an earlier, vetted version
of my central policy management.

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:22.580
And so that's our demo.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.320
Can we switch back
to the slides?

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:34.260
So that's basically what
we've got for you guys today.

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:37.290
The alpha is now open
for participation.

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.600
If you're interested
in participating,

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:40.890
that's the link there.

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:42.880
QR code will take
you to the same place

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:45.040
if you take a picture
of it with your phone.

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:47.250
There's just a brief form there.

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:52.020
We're trying to bring as
many people in as we can.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:54.810
So fill that out, let us
know if you're interested.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.730
OK, well, thanks a lot
for joining us, guys.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:57.360
RAY COLLINE: Thank
you very much.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:58.760
JOHN MURRAY: We'll be around.

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:01.380
And have a great conference.

